Company Overview

Metrea delivers effects-as-a-service to national security partners across five domains and more than a dozen mission areas. These include airborne ISR, electronic warfare, secure communications, aerial refueling, special air missions, aerial firefighting, and advanced simulation. We own the whole stack: designing, building, and operating turnkey capabilities that give our partners decisive, asymmetric advantage against rapidly evolving threats.

Our operating model is built around three interlocking pillars. The Support Groups provide a global shared-service - spanning people, finance, platform, operations, legal, and engagement. This frees up our Core Groups, who develop and own mission capabilities end-to-end, to focus entirely on delivery. The Market Groups apply a regional lens, ensuring that our agile and adaptable capabilities remain aligned to the wicked problems that matter most to our partners across the Americas, EMEA, and Asia-Pacific.

At the heart of our model is a simple but powerful idea: be a true partner with skin in the game. Our partners need effects, not just equipment. By owning the full stack - from the lab to the field - we are able to drive a continuous cycle of innovation that keeps our partners ahead. It's a fast-moving, intellectually demanding environment where talented people are given real responsibility, work on problems that matter, and contribute to an enterprise that is growing quickly and deliberately.

Headquartered in Washington, DC, with facilities across the United States, the United Kingdom, as well as Continental Europe and Asia-Pacific.

Group&nbsp;Overview

Air Mobility Group (AIRMOB)   is responsible for   developing and delivering   Metrea ’ s   aerial refueling (AAR) capabilities, ensuring the readiness and operational effectiveness of the tanker fleet. AIRMOB oversees fleet management, maintenance, and airworthiness, working to sustain and enhance refueling operations. It manages the integration of new processes and technologies, ensuring that tanker   aircraft   remain   mission-ready through effective   logistics , maintenance oversight, and regulatory compliance. By coordinating across various teams, AIRMOB ensures the fleet meets both current and future operational needs, supporting a range of specialized air mobility missions.     &nbsp;

Position Summary

The Part-time Crew Scheduler serves as one of the primary points of contact for crew scheduling and operational coordination supporting AAR operations. This role is responsible for overseeing crew deployment strategy, ensuring regulatory compliance, and making real-time operational decisions to support mission execution. &nbsp;

The Crew Scheduler exercises independent judgment in aligning crew resources with mission requirements, balancing regulatory compliance, safety, and operational priorities. In advance of mission planning, this role evaluates crew qualifications, readiness, and availability to ensure operational integrity. &nbsp;

What You'll Do

• Lead and oversee development of flight crew schedules in alignment with FAA regulations, operational priorities, and company policies &nbsp;

• Evaluate crew availability, qualifications, and readiness to determine appropriate mission assignments &nbsp;

• Exercise independent judgment in resolving complex scheduling conflicts, irregular operations, and resource constraints &nbsp;

• Serve as a central point of coordination for flight operations, partnering with pilots, AROs, and cross-functional teams &nbsp;

• Ensure compliance with FAA regulations, safety standards, and company procedures through proactive monitoring and decision-making &nbsp;

• Analyze crew utilization, duty/rest compliance, and operational demands to optimize scheduling outcomes &nbsp;

• Anticipate and mitigate operational risks by adjusting crew plans based on changing mission requirements and constraints &nbsp;

